<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Via Cassia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Via Cassia was a Roman road running north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a>, past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutri">Sutri</a>, Viterbo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and beyond. Built probably in the 2nd century BCE, it remained a main route between Rome and Tuscany in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, when parts of it carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a> pilgrim road.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/via-cassia.jpg" alt="Via Cassia" width="320" height="415" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: it:Utente:EH101 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Viae_Cassia_Aurelia_Clodia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
